        "context": "docs / Memory",
        "route": "/docs/language/memory/#synchronization",
        "text": "The standard library provides the usual memory and synchronization primitives on top of this unified memory system. The full details are documented in the library, but the basics should be familiar to anyone with a systems level background. This is also where the @unsafe actually lives: a Mutex mutates through a non exclusive receiver via UnsafeCell and atomics, and its guard manufactures the &exclusive T that the runtime lock (not the borrow checker) guarantees. Primitive Behavior Box unique heap ownership for Owned , with deterministic drop when T: Drop Rc local shared ownership of Owned , non atomic refcount, not transferable across Workers Arc shared ownership of Owned , atomic refcount, transferable as shared Arc of SharedSafe T Cell local interior mutation by value, for small Copy like state RefCell local runtime borrow checking for cases static borrowing cannot express cleanly Atomic lock free scalar storage with explicit ordering and scope AsyncMutex local mutual exclusion that suspends the current async task, not the Worker Mutex shared mutual exclusion backed by atomics and runtime wait/wake support Because ownership and placement are part of our type system, and we can query and gate based on contextual type information using regular TypeScript algebra, we have a lot of flexibility and gain some nice ergonomics. For example, we can provide ergonomic context aware aliases that conform to the way they are used: type Ref = PlaceIn extends \"shared\" ? Arc : Rc ; type Lock = PlaceIn extends \"shared\" ? Mutex : AsyncMutex ;",
